---
scope: src/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta
intent: >
  buildVersionsMeta extracts git commit metadata for each methodology doc
  from the MCP server's own repo history and returns it as a JSON object
  keyed by doc slug. The upgrade orchestrator writes this as
  .aide/docs/versions.json alongside the docs themselves, giving consuming
  projects citation data (publication date, source commit, previous commit)
  without needing GitHub API access. The data comes entirely from local
  git log — no network calls, no external APIs.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Returns a Record<string, VersionMeta> where keys are doc slugs
      (filename without .md extension, e.g. "aide-spec", "index") and
      values contain publishedAt (ISO 8601 author timestamp), sourceCommit
      (7-char short SHA), and optionally previousCommit (7-char short SHA
      of the prior commit touching that file, omitted if no prior version).
    - Git history is queried against the MCP server's own repo (resolved
      from import.meta.url, same as initContent), not the host project.
      The command is git log --follow --format="%H %aI" -2 -- <path> for
      each methodology doc.
    - When a doc has never been committed (new file not yet tracked), that
      slug is omitted from the output rather than throwing.
    - The upgrade orchestrator writes the JSON as .aide/docs/versions.json
      using compareFile (same byte-comparison + write-gate pattern as all
      other methodology artifacts), so it participates in dry-run preview
      and is overwritten on every confirm.
    - The versions.json entry appears in the upgrade output with the same
      prefix symbol and status reporting as other methodology files.
  undesired:
    - Network calls or GitHub API access. All data comes from local git.
    - Querying git history from the host project's repo. The host's git
      log tracks sync operations, not real document revisions. The source
      of truth is this repo's history.
    - Crashing when git is unavailable or a doc has no commits. Graceful
      degradation: return an empty object if git fails entirely, omit
      individual slugs that have no history.
    - A versions.json that contains entries for non-methodology files
      (commands, agents, skills). Only the docs in the .aide/docs/ hub
      get version metadata.
---
